{{also|cavalcadé}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2006|October|17}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|cavalcade}}, from {{der|en|fro|cavalcade}}, from {{der|en|roa-oit|cavalcata}}, from {{m|it|cavalcare|t=to ride on horseback}}, from {{der|en|ML.|caballicō}}, from {{der|en|VL.|caballus|t=horse}}. {{doublet|en|chevauchee}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkævəlˌkeɪd/|/ˌkævəlˈkeɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cavalcade.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-cavalcade.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪd|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|collective}} A [[procession]] of [[rider]]s, [[vehicle]]s, [[ship]]s, etc.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|month=November and December|author=O. S. Nock|title=Twenty-Four Hours at York—2|journal=Railway Magazine|pages=357–358|text=To the onlooker, and particularly to those whose memories go back to pre-grouping days, the modern '''cavalcade''' of "V2s" and "B1s" is apt to become a little monotonous; but to any running man the general utility characteristics of these two classes are a perfect godsend at times of exceptional pressure, when it is often a case of "first in—first out" with locomotive allocations at sheds.}}
#: {{syn|en|company}}
# A [[ceremonial]] [[parade]].
#: {{syn|en|parade|procession}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=For the first three hours all went as well as could be expected, and then an accident happened that nearly lost us the pleasure of the company of our venerable friend Billali, whose litter was leading the '''cavalcade'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1929|author=[[w:M. Barnard Eldershaw|M. Barnard Eldershaw]]|title=[[w:A House is Built|A House Is Built]]|section=Chapter IX, Section iii
|passage=In the second row of the '''cavalcade''' were Francie, Fanny's god-daughter, now thirteen years old and already elegant in long frilled pantalettes, tartan skirts, and a leghorn hat with streamers, …}}
# A [[trail ride]], usually more than one day long.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=[[w:Robert Barr (writer)|Robert Barr]]
|title=Lord Stranleigh Abroad
|chapter=5|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1796924W
|passage=Stranleigh found no difficulty in getting a '''cavalcade''' together at Bleacher’s station, an amazingly long distance west of New York.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} An often [[dramatic]] [[series]] or [[chain]] of [[event]]s or things.
#: {{syn|en|chain|series}}
#: {{ux|en|As soon as I visited this website, a '''cavalcade''' of dialog boxes started to appear on my screen; that's when I realized my computer was infected with a virus.}}

