===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[move]] as part of a [[series]] or [[group]], such as [[marcher]]s in a [[parade]] or [[snow]] in an [[avalanche]], especially in large numbers or in a [[chaotic]] or [[dangerous]] fashion
#* '''1725''', John Windhus, “A Journey to Mequinez”, in John Pinkerton, ''The Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels'', Volume 15, Longman et al. (1814), [http://books.google.com/books?id=2zYyAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA478&dq=cavalcading page 478]:
#*: Great numbers of horse were still '''cavalcading''', but {{...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1866|author={{w|Elizabeth Charles}}|title=The Draytons and the Davenants|publisher=M. W. Dodd|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=N4_DT8KIGWEC&pg=PA349&dq=cavalcading|pages=348-9
|passage={{...}} although for the most part he believed the devil was too good a general to let his soldiers waste their time in '''cavalcading''' about on broom-sticks.}}

